											<div id="mw-content-text" class="mw-body-content"><!-- Google Tag Manager (noscript) -->

<!-- End Google Tag Manager (noscript) -->
<div class="mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output" lang="en" dir="ltr"><p><b>Internet aesthetics</b> are a form of online expression where users curate and share images and videos to evoke a specific mood or feeling. They are an online phenomenon, distinct from traditional art movements, though the two terms are often used interchangeably in an online context. These aesthetics are primarily found on social media platforms like Tumblr, Pinterest, and TikTok. Users compile collections of media (including images, text snippets, fashion, and music) into moodboards, playlists, and videos. These digital collections are often driven by themes of wish-fulfillment, nostalgia, or escapism. While their primary existence is digital, internet aesthetics can influence a user's real-life choices, such as their fashion, home decor, and lifestyle. When a popular internet aesthetic with tangible elements, like <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Cottagecore" title="Cottagecore">Cottagecore</a>, develops an active, real-life community with shared interests, it may evolve into a subculture.
</p>
<h2 id="History" class="open-section" section="1" aria-controls="History-collapsible-section" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="true" role="button"><div class="section-header-label">History</div><a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Category:Internet_Aesthetics?action=edit&amp;section=1" class="section-edit-link section-edit-link__hidden" aria-label="Edit"><svg class="wds-icon wds-icon-small"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#wds-icons-pencil-small"></use></svg></a><div class="vertical-separator"></div><button class="chevron-wrapper" aria-controls="History-collapsible-section" aria-label="Collapse" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="true"><svg class="wds-icon wds-icon-small chevron"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#wds-icons-menu-control-small"></use></svg></button></h2><section id="History-collapsible-section" class="mobile-hidden">
</section><div class="mw-collapsible mw-collapsed mw-made-collapsible" data-expandtext="show" data-collapsetext="close"><button type="button" class="mw-collapsible-toggle mw-collapsible-toggle-default mw-collapsible-toggle-collapsed" aria-expanded="false" tabindex="0"><span class="mw-collapsible-text">show</span></button>
<div class="mw-collapsible-content" style="display: none;">
<h3><span class="mw-headline" id="Precursors_to_Internet_Aesthetics">Precursors to Internet Aesthetics</span></h3>
<p>While the term "internet aesthetic" is a modern concept, its roots can be traced to earlier online communities and literary movements. The "<a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/-punk" class="mw-redirect" title="-punk">-punk</a>" naming convention, which is prevalent in many niche literary genres and aesthetics, originated in the 1980s with <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Cyberpunk" title="Cyberpunk">Cyberpunk</a>. This literary genre, coined by writer Bruce Bethke in 1983, depicted dystopian futures driven by technology. It later grew into a visual aesthetic that influenced films, anime, and an early online community on Usenet, an early precursor to modern forums. This naming convention would later be adopted by aesthetics like Seapunk and Slimepunk. The "<a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/-core" class="mw-redirect" title="-core">-core</a>]" suffix, deriving from "hardcore punk," and "<a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/-wave" class="mw-redirect" title="-wave">-wave</a>" from "<a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/New_wave" class="mw-redirect" title="New wave">new wave</a>" were also adopted to define a new generation of internet aesthetics.
</p><p>In the late 1990s and early 2000s, early internet subcultures emerged with a strong visual identity. <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Mallgoth" title="Mallgoth">Mallgoth</a> is a subculture that mixed <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Goth" title="Goth">goth</a> fashion with <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Nu-metal" class="mw-redirect" title="Nu-metal">nu-metal</a> and <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Emo" title="Emo">emo</a> music, gaining a large following on MySpace and PureVolume. This era also saw the rise of <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Scene" title="Scene">Scene Kids</a> and the fashion of <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Indie_Sleaze" title="Indie Sleaze">Indie Sleaze</a>, styles that would later be identified as early internet aesthetics.
</p>
<h3><span class="mw-headline" id="Evolution_and_Proliferation">Evolution and Proliferation</span></h3>
<p>The rise of online microgenres and blogs in the late 2000s was a key development for internet aesthetics. One of the first widely recognized internet aesthetics, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Seapunk" title="Seapunk">Seapunk</a>, was coined on Twitter in 2011 and quickly spread on Tumblr. It was defined by its blend of 1990s web iconography and ocean motifs. This was followed by <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Vaporwave" title="Vaporwave">Vaporwave</a>, a highly influential music and visual aesthetic from the early 2010s that used retro imagery, glitch art, and early internet nostalgia to critique consumer culture.
</p><p>As social media shifted from <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/2014_Tumblr" title="2014 Tumblr">Tumblr</a> to platforms like <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/2020_TikTok" class="mw-redirect" title="2020 TikTok">TikTok</a> and Instagram in the late 2010s, new aesthetics emerged from Generation Z. The COVID-19 lockdowns are believed to have accelerated this trend. Aesthetics such as <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Cottagecore" title="Cottagecore">Cottagecore</a> and <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Dark_Academia" title="Dark Academia">Dark Academia</a> gained prominence as lifestyle communities, sometimes linked to a rejection of urban life and a desire for escapism. Other aesthetics like <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Kidcore" title="Kidcore">Kidcore</a>, <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Weirdcore" title="Weirdcore">Weirdcore</a>, and <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Goblincore" title="Goblincore">Goblincore</a> also rose in popularity.
</p>
<h2 id="Distinctions_from_Other_Concepts" class="open-section" section="4" aria-controls="Distinctions_from_Other_Concepts-collapsible-section" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="true" role="button"><div class="section-header-label">Distinctions from Other Concepts</div><a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Category:Internet_Aesthetics?action=edit&amp;section=4" class="section-edit-link section-edit-link__hidden" aria-label="Edit"><svg class="wds-icon wds-icon-small"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#wds-icons-pencil-small"></use></svg></a><div class="vertical-separator"></div><button class="chevron-wrapper" aria-controls="Distinctions_from_Other_Concepts-collapsible-section" aria-label="Collapse" aria-pressed="false" aria-expanded="true"><svg class="wds-icon wds-icon-small chevron"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#wds-icons-menu-control-small"></use></svg></button></h2><section id="Distinctions_from_Other_Concepts-collapsible-section" class="mobile-hidden">
</section><h3><span id="Distinction_from_.22Aesthetics.22"></span><span class="mw-headline" id="Distinction_from_&quot;Aesthetics&quot;">Distinction from "Aesthetics"</span></h3>
<p>The term "aesthetic" has a formal meaning as a branch of philosophy that studies beauty and art. In this traditional sense, aesthetics are defined by specific artistic principles and philosophies, such as <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Romanticism" title="Romanticism">Romanticism</a> or <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Art_Nouveau" title="Art Nouveau">Art Nouveau</a>. While all internet aesthetics are a type of aesthetic, not all aesthetics are internet aesthetics. Art movements, architecture, fashion, and other forms of creative expression all had distinct aesthetics long before the internet.
</p>
<h3><span id="Distinction_from_.22Subcultures.22"></span><span class="mw-headline" id="Distinction_from_&quot;Subcultures&quot;">Distinction from "Subcultures"</span></h3>
<p>Internet aesthetics and subcultures are often confused in online discourse but are fundamentally different. Internet aesthetics are primarily visual and exist on social media. In contrast, subcultures involve a deeper level of participation that extends into real life, with shared values, beliefs, and practices. An example often used is that you don't just "dress <a href="https://aesthetics.fandom.com/wiki/Punk" title="Punk">punk</a>," you are punk, a statement that implies a deeper, real-world community and identity. The term "internet subculture" is used to describe communities that form and exist primarily online, pre-dating the concept of internet aesthetics.
</p>
</div></div>
